WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.529 --> 00:00:03.529
(suspenseful music)

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.300
- [Instructor] Welcome to
Part 6 of your training.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.550
Let's meet the device
you'll be working with.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:13.970
This is Polybius.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:17.090
On the outside, it may look
like an ordinary arcade cabinet.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:18.000
But on the inside,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.400
Polybius holds advanced
surveillance systems,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.323
perfect for tracking user behavior.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.310
Careful! Users may experience
some slight side effects.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.153
The Polybius machine, the
future of covert surveillance.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.380
- [Narrator] Between the 1950s and 1970s,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.110
the CIA was experimenting
with alternative ways

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.480
to weaken the enemy.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.770
The program was called Project MKUltra.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.310
The CIA tested on unaware subjects,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.720
using hypnosis and psychoactive drugs

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.170
in order to find the perfect
interrogation method.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.790
In 1976, the secret program
was exposed to the public.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.560
The revelation spurred
countless conspiracy theories

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.080
that film producers
were quick to pull from.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.120
Movies like WarGames
and The Last Starfighter

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.800
combined paranoia thrills
and video game sensibilities.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.040
But movies weren't the only
things inspired by MKUltra.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.460
Beyond the theater marquees,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.650
one of the biggest video game
urban legends was brewing.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.250
And it all started with a
mysterious arcade cabinet

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:21.633
that suddenly appeared in
the quiet town of Portland.

00:01:22.537 --> 00:01:25.454
(upbeat pop music)

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.420
This is Logan Bowden.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.560
- I am Director of Operations here

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.293
at QuarterWorld Arcade in Portland Oregon.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.590
- [Narrator] Logan loves urban legends.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:38.423
- Oh, yeah, I love 'em.

00:01:38.423 --> 00:01:40.410
- [Narrator] And he's kind
of become a connoisseur

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:44.140
of the weird and wonderful
myths from Portland's past.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.940
- I've been gathering the local lore.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.940
As Portland goes, you quickly learn a lot

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.050
about what Portland was and
the folklore that surrounds it.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.790
- [Narrator] Folklore like Polybius,

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.730
Portland's most infamous legend,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.480
which he learned about while
watching TV with a friend.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.500
- We were watching “The Simpsons” episode

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.830
that has the Polybius in the background.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:07.420
And he started laughing.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.530
And I had no idea what
he was talking about,

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:10.573
so he filled me in.

00:02:11.985 --> 00:02:14.270
(suspenseful music)

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:18.770
The legend of Polybius, the
short and skinny of it is,

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:23.770
around 1981, this arcade cabinet
appeared in a few arcades

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.933
in the outskirts of Portland.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.930
Now if you played this game,

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.263
it was said that you would have seizures,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.813
fits of amnesia,

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.523
night terrors,

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.220
and also just a complete desire

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.043
to continue playing this game.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.090
- [Narrator] So why would
someone inadvertently

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.913
create a video game that
had all these effects?

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.740
Perhaps, it was on purpose.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.030
- Supposedly, every couple of weeks,

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:01.030
these strange men in
black suits would show up.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.060
And they wouldn't take any
money out of the machines,

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.720
they would just open up the back,

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.173
supposedly, maybe, taking
some readings of data,

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.543
perhaps a continuation of MKUltra.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:17.500
And then, after about two months,

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.100
just as mysteriously as they appeared,

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.283
poof, disappeared, and
they've never been seen since.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.650
- [Narrator] And so a myth was born,

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.080
and with it came a legion
of believers and skeptics

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.346
trying to decipher fact from fiction.

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:35.530
(rhythmic electronic music)

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:37.160
This is Brian Dunning.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.890
He makes a living debunking
outlandish stories

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:42.010
and has heard many tall tales.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.560
So Brian, tell us why
the story of Polybius

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.090
was a little different?

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:48.610
- It turned out, kind
of much to my surprise,

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:51.484
that just about every piece of
evidence supporting the story

00:03:51.484 --> 00:03:52.693
was true.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.210
First, two kids actually
did get knocked out

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:01.453
playing video games in
Portland, Oregon in 1981.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.840
Second, 10 days after that,

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.830
the government actually
was raiding arcades

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.093
and taking games out.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:14.070
Third, we know the games
were used by the government

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:14.903
for training.

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.760
And finally, there was
a game called Poly-Play.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.460
It was a case where this
game was physically removed

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:25.273
from every arcade in which it existed.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:27.670
- [Narrator] So it seems like

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.700
all the pieces of the puzzle exist,

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.623
leading to a confirmation
of the legend, right Brian?

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.230
- Arcade culture is
extensively documented.

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:37.450
They had trade magazines.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.080
They had consumer magazines.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.463
And in all of those issues,
throughout the entire 1980s,

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.490
there is not a single mention anywhere

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.620
of a game called Polybius.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.163
We know for a fact that it did not exist.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:55.290
- [Narrator] But proprietors of the legend

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.886
weren't quite willing to let the myth die.

00:04:57.886 --> 00:05:01.136
(dramatic organ music)

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.150
So they began to design

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.263
different elements of the game themselves.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.270
School teacher and
dedicated arcade builder,

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.750
Paul Santa Cruz, is working hard

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.023
on bringing the legend to life.

00:05:18.095 --> 00:05:21.345
(dramatic organ music)

00:05:26.032 --> 00:05:27.367
- [Paul] One day, it
kinda popped in my head,

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:30.050
"You know what, I'm
gonna make a Polybius."

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.840
- [Narrator] But how, exactly,
do you make a Polybius?

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:36.050
Well in 2007, an underground
community of gamers

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.390
actually developed a hypnotic video game

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:41.180
and marquee graphics inspired by the myth.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:44.010
It's what they thought the
game could actually look like.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.630
Paul decided to take the
work one step further

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.123
and build a fully-custom arcade cabinet.

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:51.990
- Everything was there that I needed.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:52.840
The graphics were there.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:54.710
The game was there.

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:57.923
So maybe the cabinet wanted to
exist in real space and time.

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:03.330
I wanted people to see it

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:04.710
and stop and turn to their friends

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.910
and say, "Wait a minute,
is that really it?"

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.580
I wanted it to be part of that mythos.

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:17.020
(rhythmic electronic music)

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:18.160
- [Narrator] So there you have it,

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.500
the story of how a video game legend

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:21.933
became an arcade reality.

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:24.840
- I don't think it matters at all

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.250
that the legend is true or false.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:28.903
It's just a fun thing to follow.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:30.990
- I had a lot of people tell me

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:31.823
the cabinet was cool

00:06:31.823 --> 00:06:33.100
and didn't have any interest in playing it

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:34.210
because they weren't gamers.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:37.000
But they loved the fact
that this thing existed.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.683
- If Polybius didn't exist, it does now,

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:43.330
minus the seizures and amnesia.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:47.993
You're not gonna, like, be a
sleeper cell, or are you?

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.050
(rhythmic music)

